From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 07:59:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] Take speeds as the derivative of the reported longitude
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every body's speed was a central difference over ±0.25 day. For the Moon
that leaves up to 8.9″ a day of error at perigee against Swiss Ephemeris'
own speeds, 7.55″ of it from the step alone (audit ledger, finding 1g). The
Saturn return scan took natal Saturn's direction from a separate ±1-day
difference, so at a station the two could disagree for about 40 s.

The planets and the Moon now use ±0.001 day (86.4 s). That differentiates
the longitude the engine reports with no frame bookkeeping. The Moon's step
error is left at 0.0015″ a day, and each body still costs two extra
evaluations. The true node keeps ±0.25 day: its short-period noise makes
smaller steps worse against Swiss' true node, and its gates depend on it.
computeSaturnReturns takes the natal direction from the same speed.

Tests: every body's speed matches a ±1e-4-day difference of its longitude
to 0.01″ a day, every third day of 2025. Node speed equals the six-hour
difference. retrograde matches the sign of the speed. The Saturn return
scan agrees with the chart on both sides of the 2026-07-26 station. The
bounds test's ephemeris mock gains the two new names.

